mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{Q rSub { size 8{c} } } {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span>, and to define the efficiency of a cyclical heat engine. As noted, a cyclical process brings the system back to its original condition at the end of every cycle. Such a system's internal energy <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mi>U</mi></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> is the same at the beginning and end of every cycle—that is, <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>Δ</mn><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>Δ</mn><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{ΔU=0} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span>. The first law of thermodynamics states that</p><div data-type="equation" id="eip-36"><math display="block"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>Δ</mn><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">−</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mtext>,</mtext><mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>Δ</mn><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">−</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mtext>,</mtext><mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{ΔU=Q - W} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math><div class="os-equation-number">
  <span class="os-number">15.22</span>
</div>
</div>
    <p id="import-auto-id1169736629320">where <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{Q} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> is the <em data-effect="italics">net</em> heat transfer during the cycle (<span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">−</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">−</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{Q=Q rSub { size 8{h} }  - Q rSub { size 8{c} } } {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span>) and <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{W} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> is the net work done by the system. Since <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>Δ</mn><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>Δ</mn><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{ΔU=0} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> for a complete cycle, we have</p><div data-type="equation" id="eip-946"><math display="block"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">−</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">−</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{0=Q - W} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math><div class="os-equation-number">
  <span class="os-number">15.23</span>
</div>
</div>
    <p id="import-auto-id1169736584184">so that</p>
    <div data-type="equation" id="eip-118"><math display="block"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{W=Q} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math><div class="os-equation-number">
  <span class="os-number">15.24</span>
</div>
</div>
    <p id="import-auto-id1169738092064">Thus the net work done by the system equals the net heat transfer into the system, or</p>
    <div data-type="equation" id="eip-263"><math display="block"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">−</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mtext>(cyclical process),</mtext></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mtext>h</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">−</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mtext>(cyclical process),</mtext></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{W=Q rSub { size 8{h} }  - Q rSub { size 8{c} } } {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math><div class="os-equation-number">
  <span class="os-number">15.25</span>
</div>
</div><p id="import-auto-id1169737992344">just as shown schematically in <a href="15-3-introduction-to-the-second-law-of-thermodynamics-heat-engines-and-their-efficiency#import-auto-id1169736606615" class="autogenerated-content" aria-label="View Figure 15.18">Figure 15.18</a>(b). The problem is that in all processes, there is some heat transfer <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{Q rSub { size 8{c} } } {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> to the environment—and usually a very significant amount at that.</p><p id="import-auto-id1169737966671">In the conversion of energy to work, we are always faced with the problem of getting less out than we put in. We define <em data-effect="italics">conversion efficiency</em> <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle fontstyle="italic"><mrow><mtext>Eff</mtext></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle fontstyle="italic"><mrow><mtext>Eff</mtext></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0"> size 12{ ital "Eff"} {}</annotation></semantics></annotation-xml></semantics